@module rooms ; The room data, Unrle, the room builder and the room changes
$9AE2 b Grid0 9 ; Grid 0's record (GridNumber 0: the palm beach, the pyramid's steps and the barrier): 25 x 9 blocks, start window 0, 0, the room table Grid0Rooms, map offset 0
> A grid record, 9 bytes, as in stage 1: +0 the width and +1 the height in blocks, +2/+3 the start window's block column and row (StartGrid $A5CC copies them to RoomColumn and RoomRow; the grid passages rewrite them, Grid1 +2 at ReturnToGrid1 $8D23 and Grid8 +2 at ReturnToGrid8 $8CE0), +4/+5 the room table, +6/+7 the grid's offset in the map section of the unpacked graphics, +8 the rooms a grid row of rooms (meaningful only in a grid of more than one row of rooms: Grid1). A room is a window of 14 x 9 blocks; the windows step 11 block columns and 6 block rows. RoomGrid points at the record of the hero's grid.
> Stage 4 has nine grids, where stage 1 has three. They are named by the GridNumber ($FE47) the grid passages give them (EnterGrid1 $8B8C to EnterGrid10 $969C), not by their order: the records lie in the order 0, 9, 2, 1, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10 (GridNumber 6 and 7 do not exist); tools/fig_rooms.py numbers them 0-8 in that order. Stage 4 keeps their nine records together, then the nine room tables (Grid0Rooms $9B33), then the nine placement lists (Grid0Placements $9B50): stage 1 puts each room table after its record. The resource is rooms-split.txt (tools/res_stage4.py), in rooms.txt's language; tools/fig_rooms.py draws the map and every room from it (docs/img/maps/stage4.png, stage4-rooms.png).
> Read in s4-right (RoomCells $9898, StartGrid $A5CC, LeaveRoomDown $A06A, the hero's room checks); the start columns written only by the grid passages, which s4-right did not run.
